= Memory =

== Overview ==
'''Memory''' is the capacity and set of processes by which past experience influences later cognition or behavior.

Memory involves more than storage. Researchers distinguish encoding, consolidation or retention, retrieval, reconstruction, and use.

== Multiple memory systems ==
Memory is commonly divided into partly distinct systems and processes, including working memory, episodic memory, semantic memory, procedural memory, perceptual learning, conditioning, and short- and long-term retention.

These divisions are theoretical and biological distinctions, not folders in a literal archive.

== Encoding ==
Encoding is the transformation of experience into a form that can influence later processing.

[[Attention]], organization, prior knowledge, emotion, and goals affect what is encoded.

== Consolidation and retrieval ==
Some memory traces become more stable over time through biological and cognitive processes.

Retrieval is not simple playback. Remembering is cue-dependent and reconstructive.

== Forgetting ==
Forgetting can result from weak encoding, interference, retrieval failure, updating, or active control.

Failure to recall does not prove that a memory trace was completely erased.

== Accuracy and confidence ==
Memory confidence and memory accuracy can diverge.

Eyewitness and misinformation research demonstrate that vivid recollection is not an infallible record.

== Memory and learning ==
[[Learning]] produces relatively durable changes through experience.

Memory is one mechanism and outcome through which learning can be expressed, but learning also includes skill, conditioning, expectation, and adaptive behavior.

